---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
const repo = "https://github.com/tidus747/planacity";
const windows =
  'git clone https://github.com/tidus747/planacity.git\ncd planacity\npy -3.11 -m venv .venv\n.venv\\Scripts\\python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"\n.venv\\Scripts\\python -m planacity';
const linux =
  "git clone https://github.com/tidus747/planacity.git\ncd planacity\npython3 -m venv .venv\n.venv/bin/python -m pip install -e '.[dev]'\n.venv/bin/python -m planacity";
---

<Page
  title="Get started"
  description="Run the Planacity source preview, open the fictional example, and build your first local program plan."
>
  <div class="shell">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">GET STARTED</p>
      <h1>Your first program plan.</h1>
      <p>
        Run the desktop preview, open a small example, and explore at your own
        pace.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="doc-grid">
      <aside class="doc-nav" aria-label="On this page">
        <p>On this page</p>
        <a href="#install">Run from source</a>
        <a href="#example">Open the example</a>
        <a href="#first-plan">Make it your own</a>
        <a href="#guides">Workflow guides</a>
        <a href="#status">Preview status</a>
      </aside>
      <div class="prose">
        <div class="notice">
          <strong>A development preview, built in the open.</strong>
          <p>
            There is no stable installer yet. You will need Git and Python 3.11
            or newer. Installing dependencies requires an internet connection;
            planning afterwards works offline.
          </p>
        </div>
        <section id="install">
          <h2>01. Run from source</h2>
          <p>Windows is the initial desktop target. In PowerShell, run:</p>
          <pre><code>{windows}</code></pre>
          <p>On Linux, with Python 3.11 or newer:</p>
          <pre><code>{linux}</code></pre>
          <p>
            The application opens as a desktop window. Keep your plans in local{" "}
            <code>.planacity</code> files. No account or web server is needed.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section id="example">
          <h2>02. Open the example</h2>
          <p>
            Choose <strong>File -&gt; Restore JSON backup...</strong> and open{" "}
            <code>examples/aurora.planacity.json</code> from the repository you
            just cloned.
          </p>
          <p>
            Aurora is a fictional test-bench program with work, people, and
            dates. Use <strong>File -&gt; Save As...</strong> to save your own{" "}
            <code>.planacity</code> copy before experimenting.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/examples/aurora.planacity.json`}>
              Inspect the example on GitHub
            </a>
          </p>
        </section>
        <section id="first-plan">
          <h2>03. Make it your own</h2>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <strong>Structure the work.</strong> In Plan, add Epics, Tasks,
              and Subtasks. Use F2 to edit a title, estimate, or date.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>See the schedule.</strong> Open Timeline, choose a scale,
              and filter by work type or WorkGroup. Dependency arrows show
              relationships; automatic dependency enforcement is still planned.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Set up your team.</strong> In People, create work
              calendars and assign them explicitly. Add dated availability.
              There is no assumed 8-hour day.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Reserve and allocate.</strong> Use Planning -&gt; Reserve
              capacity... for recurring duties. In Plan, select work and open
              Work allocations... to split its hours between people.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Save and continue.</strong> Save the project, close it,
              and reopen it. Export a JSON backup for a separate copy.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Light and dark appearance are available in the app's sidebar. For
            Days or Weeks estimates, use{" "}
            <strong>Planning -&gt; Estimate units...</strong> and choose a
            reference calendar.
          </p>
        </section>
        <section id="guides">
          <h2>Go a little deeper</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/getting-started.md`}>
                Full editing and keyboard guide
              </a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/jira-import.md`}>
                Import Jira CSV and preserve a baseline
              </a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/jira-export.md`}>
                Export agreed work to Jira CSV
              </a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/capacity-model.md`}>
                Work calendars and availability
              </a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/capacity-wizards.md`}>
                Recurring meetings and front-office reservations
              </a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/allocation-model.md`}>
                Split work between people
              </a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>
        <section id="status">
          <h2>Know the current boundaries</h2>
          <p>
            Current source is <strong>0.4.0.dev0</strong>. Calendars,
            availability, recurring reservations, and allocations are editable,
            but they do not yet form one combined remaining-capacity
            calculation.
          </p>
          <p>
            Projects save in schema 7. Older schemas remain readable; keep a
            backup or use Save As if an older build must still open your
            original file.
          </p>
          <p>
            Next: stronger dependency checks, hierarchy effort totals, and
            consistent capacity views.{" "}
            <a href={`${base}/roadmap/`}>See the roadmap</a>, or{" "}
            <a href={`${repo}/issues`}>report what you find</a>.
          </p>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Page>
